    <strong>Context for analysts:</strong> The balanced sample across gender, school type, and district supports generalisable conclusions. 
    No single district or demographic group dominates, so observed differences are more likely to reflect real structural factors than sampling bias.
  </div>
</section>

<section id="academic">
  <h2>Academic Performance Analysis</h2>
  <p>HSC Results in the sample range from <strong>3.00 to 5.00</strong> with a mean of <strong>4.01</strong> (SD = 0.59). 
  The distribution is roughly symmetric with a slight negative skew, driven by the floor effect at 3.00. 
  Every student in the dataset passed (≥ 2.0), so policy attention should shift from pass/fail to quality tiers.</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['hsc_distribution']}" alt="HSC Distribution"></div>
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['performance_class']}" alt="Performance Classification"></div>
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Performance Tier</th><th class="num">Count</th><th class="num">Share</th><th>Definition</th></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-green">Excellent</span></td><td class="num">268</td><td class="num">26.8%</td><td>HSC GPA ≥ 4.5</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-amber">Good</span></td><td class="num">238</td><td class="num">23.8%</td><td>HSC GPA 4.0–4.49</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-blue">Average</span></td><td class="num">494</td><td class="num">49.4%</td><td>HSC GPA 3.0–3.99</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-red">Poor</span></td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0.0%</td><td>HSC GPA &lt; 3.0</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>GPA Progression: A System Under Stress</h3>
  <p>The longitudinal GPA data tells a powerful story. Average Previous GPA was 3.99, rising to 4.26 at SSC, then falling back to 4.01 at HSC. 
  The net change from Previous GPA to HSC is a marginal +0.018, but this masks two opposing forces:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>50.7%</strong> of students improved from their Previous GPA to HSC.</li>
    <li><strong>48.4%</strong> declined over the same period.</li>
    <li><strong>62.6%</strong> scored lower on HSC than on SSC — a decline from the immediately preceding exam.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['gpa_progression']}" alt="GPA Progression"></div>

  <div class="alert">
    <strong>Critical finding:</strong> The SSC-to-HSC drop is the most alarming signal in the dataset. 
    It suggests that whatever factors boost performance at SSC (coaching intensity, exam familiarity, curriculum alignment) are not sustained into HSC. 
    Policymakers should investigate curriculum difficulty jumps, teaching quality at the higher-secondary level, and student motivation trajectories.
  </div>
</section>

<section id="socioeconomic">
  <h2>Socio-Economic Factors and Their Impact on Performance</h2>
  <p>This section examines family income, parent education, internet access, and private tuition — the levers most often discussed in education policy. 
  The data reveals that <strong>parent education</strong> produces the largest and most consistent effect, while income, internet, and tuition show surprisingly small associations with HSC outcomes.</p>

  <h3>Parent Education: The Strongest Predictor</h3>
  <p>Students whose parents completed secondary education outperform those with primary-educated parents by <strong>0.086 GPA points</strong> (Cohen's <em>d</em> = 0.146). 
  The hierarchy is clear: Secondary &gt; Higher Secondary &gt; Graduate &gt; Primary. Notably, graduate-level parent education does not confer additional benefit over secondary — a pattern that merits further qualitative investigation.</p>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['parent_education']}" alt="Parent Education"></div>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Parent Education</th><th class="num">Mean HSC GPA</th><th class="num">Students</th><th class="num">Excellent Rate</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Secondary</td><td class="num">4.050</td><td class="num">247</td><td class="num">29.6%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Higher Secondary</td><td class="num">4.035</td><td class="num">251</td><td class="num">27.9%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Graduate</td><td class="num">4.001</td><td class="num">267</td><td class="num">25.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Primary</td><td class="num">3.964</td><td class="num">235</td><td class="num">24.7%</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Effect-size interpretation:</strong> A Cohen's <em>d</em> of 0.146 is small but non-trivial in policy terms. 
    At the population level, shifting a cohort from primary to secondary parent-education exposure could lift hundreds of students into the Good or Excellent tiers.
  </div>

  <h3>Internet Access &amp; Private Tuition: Modest Gains</h3>
  <p>Both internet access and private tuition are associated with higher HSC GPAs, but the effect sizes are minimal:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Internet access:</strong> +0.017 GPA (<em>d</em> = 0.029) — essentially negligible.</li>
    <li><strong>Private tuition:</strong> +0.012 GPA (<em>d</em> = 0.020) — also negligible.</li>
    <li><strong>Both together vs neither:</strong> +0.030 GPA (<em>d</em> = 0.052) — still small.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['internet_tuition']}" alt="Internet and Tuition"></div>

  <p>The four support-profile groups are remarkably tightly clustered (range 3.99–4.02). 
  This suggests that in the current Bangladeshi context, simply having internet or tuition is not a decisive performance driver. 
  Quality of use (how students utilise internet resources, or the calibre of tuition received) likely matters far more than binary access.</p>

  <h3>Family Income: No Clear Gradient</h3>
  <p>Family income shows no linear relationship with HSC performance. The highest-performing income quartile is Q3 (mean 4.025), not Q4 (4.012). 
  The overall range across quartiles is only 0.033 GPA points (<em>d</em> = −0.018), indicating that income alone does not determine academic success in this sample.</p>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['income_quartile']}" alt="Income Quartiles"></div>

  <div class="alert">
    <strong>Policy implication:</strong> If income and binary resource access show such weak links to outcomes, 
    interventions should focus on <em>how</em> resources are used (pedagogical quality, digital literacy, parental engagement) rather than <em>whether</em> they are available.
  </div>

  <h3>Effect-Size Summary Table</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Comparison</th><th class="num">Mean Diff (GPA)</th><th class="num">Cohen's <em>d</em></th><th>Interpretation</th></tr>
'''

for name, diff, d in effects:
    interp = 'Small' if abs(d) >= 0.1 else 'Negligible'
    if abs(d) >= 0.3:
        interp = 'Small-to-medium'
    html += f'    <tr><td>{name}</td><td class="num">{diff:+.3f}</td><td class="num">{d:+.3f}</td><td>{interp}</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''  </table>
</section>

<section id="study">
  <h2>Study Behavior and Attendance Patterns</h2>
  <p>Conventional wisdom holds that more study hours and higher attendance should boost results. 
  The Bangladesh dataset partially challenges this assumption.</p>

  <h3>Study Hours: Non-Linear and Weakly Negative</h3>
  <p>The correlation between weekly study hours and HSC GPA is <strong>−0.022</strong> — essentially zero. 
  Students studying 0–10 hours per week average 4.03, while those studying 16–20 hours average 3.95. 
  The pattern is U-shaped rather than linear: moderate study loads (16–20 hours) underperform both lighter and heavier loads.</p>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['study_attendance']}" alt="Study and Attendance"></div>

  <h3>Attendance: No Predictive Power</h3>
  <p>Attendance correlates with HSC GPA at <strong>−0.014</strong>. Students in the 76–85% attendance bracket actually score highest (4.07), 
  while those with near-perfect attendance (96–100%) score lowest (3.94). This counter-intuitive finding suggests that:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Attendance may be a poor proxy for engagement in this context.</li>
    <li>Students with lower attendance may compensate with more focused self-study or private tuition.</li>
    <li>School quality (what happens <em>in</em> class) may matter more than simply being present.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Policy should move beyond "butt-in-seat" metrics. 
    Targeted, high-quality instruction and effective self-study strategies are likely more impactful than simply increasing study hours or enforcing attendance.
  </div>
</section>

<section id="regional">
  <h2>Regional Performance Disparities</h2>
  <p>Geographic inequality is the most pronounced dimension in the dataset. 
  The gap between the highest-performing district (Barisal, 4.113) and the lowest (Rangpur, 3.909) is <strong>0.204 GPA points</strong> (<em>d</em> = 0.34). 
  This is the largest effect size observed across any single factor.</p>

